The '''2018 FIFA World Cup''' ([[Russian language|Russian]]: Чемпионат мира по футболу 2018 года) was the 21st [[FIFA World Cup]], the quadrennial world championship for national [[association football|football]] teams organised by [[FIFA]]. It took place in Russia from 14 June to 15 July 2018, after the country was awarded the hosting rights in late 2010. It was the eleventh time the championships had been held in Europe and the first time they were held in [[Russia]], also featuring one or two venues, depending on definition, located across [[Boundary between Asia and Europe|the boundary]] with Asia. At an estimated cost of over $14.2 billion, it was the most expensive World Cup ever held until it was surpassed by the [[2022 FIFA World Cup|2022 World Cup]] in Qatar.<ref>{{cite web |last=Ellyatt |first=Holly |date=14 June 2018 |title=The World Cup will give Russia's economy a boost — just don't expect it to last |url=https://www.cnbc.com/2018/06/14/russia-world-cup-will-give-economy-a-boost.html |access-date=5 July 2021 |publisher=[[CNBC]] |archive-date=8 October 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221008042430/https://www.cnbc.com/2018/06/14/russia-world-cup-will-give-economy-a-boost.html |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=The Games That Cost Russia $14&nbsp;Billion: 9 Wild World Cup Money Stats |url=https://www.fool.com/slideshow/games-cost-russia-14-billion-9-wild-world-cup-money-stats/ |access-date=5 July 2021 |website=The Motley Fool |archive-date=12 November 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221112203532/https://www.fool.com/slideshow/games-cost-russia-14-billion-9-wild-world-cup-money-stats/ |url-status=live}}</ref>

The tournament phase involved 32 teams, of which 31 came through [[2018 FIFA World Cup qualification|qualifying competitions]], while the host nation [[Russia national football team|Russia]] qualified automatically. Of the 32, 20 had also appeared in the [[2014 FIFA World Cup|2014 event]], while [[Iceland national football team|Iceland]] and [[Panama national football team|Panama]] each made their debut at the World Cup. 64 matches were played in 12 venues across 11 cities. [[Germany national football team|Germany]], the defending champions, were eliminated in the group stage for the first time since [[1938 FIFA World Cup|1938]]. Host nation  [[Russia national football team|Russia]] was eliminated in the quarter-finals. In the [[2018 FIFA World Cup final|final]], [[France national football team|France]] played [[Croatia national football team|Croatia]] on 15 July at [[Luzhniki Stadium]] in [[Moscow]]. France won the match 4–2, claiming their second World Cup and becoming the fourth consecutive European winners after [[Italy national football team|Italy]] in [[2006 FIFA World Cup|2006]], [[Spain national football team|Spain]] in [[2010 FIFA World Cup|2010]], and [[Germany national football team|Germany]] in [[2014 FIFA World Cup|2014]]–a new record for teams representing the same continent.{{refn|group=note|Alternatively, if one accepts the claim that the football tournaments at the [[Football at the 1924 Summer Olympics|1924]] and [[Football at the 1928 Summer Olympics|1928 Summer Olympics]] should be counted as the first association football world championships, then in 2014 European teams repeated the three wins in a row streak achieved by South Americans earlier, as the two Olympic football tournaments and [[1930 FIFA World Cup|the first edition of the FIFA World Cup in 1930]] were [[Four stars above Uruguay's football crest|won by Uruguay]].<ref>[https://archive.org/details/httpswww.fifa.comfifaplusenarticlesthe-stars-that-adorn-la-celeste-why-uruguay-d/Akshat%20Mehrish%204%20FIFA%20article%204%20copy.jpg Akshat Mehrish's FIFA.com article on Uruguay's 4 world titles, 2023]</ref>}}

[[Croatia national football team|Croatian]] player [[Luka Modrić]] was voted the tournament's best player and awarded the [[FIFA World Cup Golden Ball|Golden Ball]]. [[England national football team|England]]'s [[Harry Kane]] won the [[FIFA World Cup Golden Boot|Golden Boot]] after scoring the most goals during the tournament with six. [[Belgium national football team|Belgium]]'s [[Thibaut Courtois]] won the [[FIFA World Cup Golden Glove|Golden Glove]], awarded to the [[Goalkeeper (association football)|goalkeeper]] with the best performance. [[Kylian Mbappé]] was voted the [[FIFA World Cup Best Young Player Award|World Cup Best Young Player]] and became the second teenager, after [[Pelé]], to score in a [[List of FIFA World Cup finals|World Cup final]]. It has been estimated that more than 3&nbsp;million people attended games during the tournament.

==Host selection==
{{Main|2018 and 2022 FIFA World Cup bids}}
[[File:Russia 2018 World Cup.jpeg|thumb|[[Andrey Arshavin]] with Russian bid personnel celebrate the awarding of the 2018 World Cup to Russia on 2 December 2010.]]
The [[2018 and 2022 FIFA World Cup bids|bidding procedure to host the 2018 and 2022 FIFA World Cup tournaments]] began in January 2009, and national associations had until 2 February 2009 to register their interest.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Goff |first=Steve |url=http://voices.washingtonpost.com/soccerinsider/2009/01/future_world_cups.html |title=Future World Cups |date=16 January 2009 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |access-date=16 January 2009 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110430153000/http://voices.washingtonpost.com/soccerinsider/2009/01/future_world_cups.html |archive-date=30 April 2011}}</ref> Initially, nine countries placed bids for the 2018 FIFA World Cup, but [[Mexico national football team|Mexico]] later withdrew from the proceedings,<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.fifa.com/newscentre/news/newsid=1109321.html |title=Mexico withdraws FIFA World Cup bid |date=29 September 2009 |access-date=10 February 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110430035641/http://www.fifa.com/newscentre/news/newsid=1109321.html |archive-date=30 April 2011 |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association}}</ref> and [[Indonesia national football team|Indonesia]]'s bid was rejected by FIFA in February 2010 after the [[Government of Indonesia|Indonesian government]] failed to submit a letter to support the bid.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/8577452.stm |title=Indonesia's bid to host the 2022 World Cup bid ends |date=19 March 2010 |access-date=19 March 2010 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100320054013/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/8577452.stm |archive-date=20 March 2010 |publisher=[[BBC News]]}}</ref> During the bidding process, the three remaining non-[[UEFA]] nations ([[Australia men's national soccer team|Australia]], [[Japan national football team|Japan]], and the [[United States men's national soccer team|United States]]) gradually withdrew from the 2018 bids, and thus all UEFA nations were ruled out of the 2022 bid. As such, there were eventually four bids for the 2018 FIFA World Cup, two of which were joint bids: [[England national football team|England]], [[Russia national football team|Russia]], [[Netherlands national football team|Netherlands]]/[[Belgium national football team|Belgium]], and [[Portuguese national football team|Portugal]]/[[Spain national football team|Spain]].<ref name="xBccj">{{Cite news |last1=Doyle |first1=Paul |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2010/dec/01/world-cup-2018-2022-zurich |title=World Cup 2018 and 2022 decision day – live! |date=2 December 2010 |work=The Guardian |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161226062244/https://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2010/dec/01/world-cup-2018-2022-zurich |archive-date=26 December 2016 |last2=Busfield |first2=Steve |location=London}}</ref>

The 22-member [[FIFA Council|FIFA Executive Committee]] convened in [[Zürich]] on 2 December 2010 to vote to select the hosts of both tournaments.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.fifa.com/aboutfifa/federation/bodies/media/newsid=983481.html |title=Combined bidding confirmed |date=20 December 2008 |access-date=20 December 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090122070321/http://www.fifa.com/aboutfifa/federation/bodies/media/newsid%3D983481.html |archive-date=22 January 2009 |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association}}</ref> Russia won the right to be the 2018 host in the second round of voting. The Portugal/Spain bid came second, and that from Belgium/Netherlands third. England, which was bidding to host its second tournament, was eliminated in the first round.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/9250585.stm |title=England miss out to Russia in 2018 World Cup Vote |date=2 December 2010 |publisher=BBC News |access-date=2 December 2010 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20101203045541/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/9250585.stm |archive-date=3 December 2010}}</ref>

