===Host selection criticism===
[[File:Russia 100 Rubles 2018 FIFA World Cup.jpg|thumb|The 100-[[Russian ruble|ruble]] commemorative banknote celebrates the 2018 FIFA World Cup. It features an image of Soviet goalkeeper [[Lev Yashin]].|252x252px]]
The choice of Russia as host was controversial. Issues included the high level of [[Racism in Russia|racism]] in Russian football,<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2010/dec/03/world-cup-2018-russian-racism |title=World Cup 2018 win raises Russian racism fears |last=Syal |first=Rajeev |date=3 December 2010 |website=The Guardian |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130920223511/http://www.theguardian.com/world/2010/dec/03/world-cup-2018-russian-racism |archive-date=20 September 2013 |access-date=20 August 2011}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/24660581 |title=Yaya Toure: Black players may boycott 2018 Russia World Cup |date=25 October 2013 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160118110813/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/24660581 |archive-date=18 January 2016}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Munro |first=Kelsey |url=https://www.sbs.com.au/news/racism-in-russia-and-fifa-s-3-step-plan-to-tackle-it |title=Racism in Russia and FIFA's 3-step plan to tackle it |date=11 June 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180612000108/https://www.sbs.com.au/news/racism-in-russia-and-fifa-s-3-step-plan-to-tackle-it |archive-date=12 June 2018 |agency=Sbs.com.au}}</ref> [[Human rights abuses in Russia|human rights abuses]] by Russian authorities,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Krivosheev |first=Denis |date=2017-06-16 |title=FIFA must challenge Russia's human rights record |url=https://www.cnn.com/2017/06/16/opinions/krivosheev-russia-human-rights/index.html |access-date=2022-12-06 |website=[[CNN]] |language=en |archive-date=6 December 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221206193600/https://www.cnn.com/2017/06/16/opinions/krivosheev-russia-human-rights/index.html |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |url=https://pen-international.org/defending-free-expression/keeping-score-2018 |title=Keeping Score 2018: Shrinking Space for Freedom of Expression in Russia |date=25 May 2018 |work=PEN International |access-date=15 March 2020 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190426032456/https://pen-international.org/defending-free-expression/keeping-score-2018 |archive-date=26 April 2019}}</ref> and [[Homophobia in Russia|discrimination]] against [[LGBT]] people in government (including [[Russian gay propaganda law|gay propaganda laws]]) along with wider Russian society.<ref>{{Cite news |last=McCormick |first=Joseph Patrick |url=http://www.pinknews.co.uk/2014/03/25/campaigns-demand-fifa-relocates-2018-world-cup-russia-due-anti-gay-law/ |title=Campaigns demand FIFA bans Russia from hosting 2018 World Cup due to anti-gay law |date=25 March 2014 |work=Pink News |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141006082031/http://www.pinknews.co.uk/2014/03/25/campaigns-demand-fifa-relocates-2018-world-cup-russia-due-anti-gay-law/ |archive-date=6 October 2014}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.advocate.com/commentary/2014/02/28/op-ed-why-fifa-needs-move-world-cup |title=Why FIFA Needs to Move the World Cup |last=Lavin |first=James |date=28 February 2014 |website=[[The Advocate (LGBT magazine)|The Advocate]] |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141006102722/http://www.advocate.com/commentary/2014/02/28/op-ed-why-fifa-needs-move-world-cup |archive-date=6 October 2014}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2018-07-12 |title=Fifa tells broadcasters to stop cutaways to ‘hot’ female fans |url=https://www.the-independent.com/sport/football/world-cup/world-cup-2018-fifa-hot-female-football-fans-crack-down-anti-sexism-campaign-a8443901.html |access-date=2026-06-29 |website=The Independent |language=en}}</ref> Russia's [[annexation of Crimea by the Russian Federation|annexation of Crimea]] had also prompted calls for the tournament to be moved.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-politics-26679542 |title=Fifa urged to rethink staging 2018 World Cup in Russia |date=21 March 2014 |publisher=BBC News |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140407092515/http://www.bbc.com/news/uk-politics-26679542 |archive-date=7 April 2014 |access-date=17 April 2014}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.express.co.uk/news/world/466357/US-calls-for-Fifa-to-drop-Russia-from-hosting-World-Cup-in-2018 |title=US calls for Fifa to drop Russia from hosting World Cup in 2018 |last=Winter |first=Stuart |date=23 March 2014 |website=[[Daily Express]] |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140416135801/http://www.express.co.uk/news/world/466357/US-calls-for-Fifa-to-drop-Russia-from-hosting-World-Cup-in-2018 |archive-date=16 April 2014 |access-date=17 April 2014}}</ref> In 2014, FIFA president [[Sepp Blatter]] stated that "the World Cup has been given and voted to Russia and we are going forward with our work".<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/0/football/26691561 |title=Sepp Blatter: Russia will host 2018 World Cup despite Crimea |date=21 March 2014 |publisher=BBC Sport |access-date=17 April 2014 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140428234840/http://www.bbc.com/sport/0/football/26691561 |archive-date=28 April 2014}}</ref>

Russia was criticised for alleged abuse of migrant labourers in the construction of World Cup venues,<ref>{{Cite news |last=Patrick Jennings |date=16 June 2017 |title=Confederations Cup: World Cup host Russia set to come under spotlight |language=en-GB |work=[[BBC Sport]] |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/40026277 |access-date=6 December 2022 |archive-date=6 December 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221206195102/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/40026277 |url-status=live}}</ref> with [[Human Rights Watch]] reporting cases where workers were left unpaid, made to work in dangerously cold conditions, or suffering reprisals for raising concerns.<ref>{{Cite news |agency=[[Reuters]] |date=2017-06-14 |title=Workers exploited at Russia's 2018 World Cup venues, reveals Human Rights Watch |url=https://www.indiatoday.in/sports/football/story/russia-fifa-2018-world-cup-human-rights-watch-982654-2017-06-14 |access-date=2022-12-06 |magazine=[[India Today]] |language=en |archive-date=6 December 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221206193600/https://www.indiatoday.in/sports/football/story/russia-fifa-2018-world-cup-human-rights-watch-982654-2017-06-14 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Editorial Board |date=2017-06-15 |title=FIFA has a human rights problem of its own making |language=en-US |newspaper=[[Washington Post]] |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/opinions/fifa-has-a-human-rights-problem-of-its-own-making/2017/06/15/728fd634-5145-11e7-b064-828ba60fbb98_story.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190504055610/https://www.washingtonpost.com/opinions/fifa-has-a-human-rights-problem-of-its-own-making/2017/06/15/728fd634-5145-11e7-b064-828ba60fbb98_story.html |archive-date=2019-05-04 |issn=0190-8286}}</ref> A few pundits claimed it was slave labour.<ref>{{cite AV media |date=23 June 2017 |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hiRYGYHbJ48 |title=Russia accused of exploiting World Cup stadium workers |publisher=[[TRT World]] |via=[[YouTube]] |access-date=20 December 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221208225516/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hiRYGYHbJ48 |archive-date=8 December 2022 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=ANNE M. PETERSON |date=2022-06-25 |title=Group asks for living wages, labor rights for 2026 World Cup |url=https://www.taiwannews.com.tw/en/news/4580832 |access-date=2022-12-06 |website=[[Taiwan News]] |publisher=[[Associated Press]] |archive-date=6 December 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221206193557/https://www.taiwannews.com.tw/en/news/4580832 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Martyn Ziegler |date=2017-03-01 |title=Russia and Fifa face 'slave labour' claims |language=en |work=[[The Times]] |url=https://www.thetimes.com/sport/football/article/russia-and-fifa-face-slave-labour-claims-b7fqf2ms7 |url-status=live |access-date=2022-12-08 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20221208222301/https://www.thetimes.co.uk/article/russia-and-fifa-face-slave-labour-claims-b7fqf2ms7 |archive-date=2022-12-08 |issn=0140-0460}}</ref> In May 2017, FIFA president [[Gianni Infantino]] admitted there had been human rights abuses of North Korean workers involved in the construction of [[Saint Petersburg]]'s [[Zenit Arena]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=[[Dan Roan]] |date=2017-11-09 |title=World Cup: Fifa urged to strengthen workers' rights in Russia & Qatar |language=en-GB |work=[[BBC Sport]] |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/41929421 |access-date=2022-12-06 |archive-date=6 December 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221206193554/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/41929421 |url-status=live}}</ref> By June 2017, at least 17 workers had died on World Cup construction sites, according to [[Building and Wood Workers' International]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=[[Associated Press]] |date=2017-06-14 |title=Workers on 2018 World Cup construction sites face abuse: Report |url=https://indianexpress.com/article/sports/football/workers-on-2018-world-cup-construction-sites-face-abuse-report-4703711/ |access-date=2022-12-06 |website=[[The Indian Express]] |language=en |archive-date=6 December 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221206193600/https://indianexpress.com/article/sports/football/workers-on-2018-world-cup-construction-sites-face-abuse-report-4703711/ |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=June 14, 2017 |title=HRW 'Red Cards' Russia, FIFA For Alleged Abuse Of World Cup Venue Builders |url=https://www.rferl.org/a/human-rights-watch-documented-cases-russian-workers-abuses-building-soccer-venues-world-cup-fifa/28547730.html |work=[[Radio Free Europe/Radio Liberty]] |language=en |access-date=8 December 2022 |archive-date=9 December 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221209043436/https://www.rferl.org/a/human-rights-watch-documented-cases-russian-workers-abuses-building-soccer-venues-world-cup-fifa/28547730.html |url-status=live}}</ref> In August, a group of eight US senators called on FIFA to consider dismissing Russia as the World Cup host if an independent investigation verified allegations of North Koreans being subjected to forced labour.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Min-joo |first=Kim |date=2017-08-03 |title=US senators urge FIFA to dismiss Russia as World Cup host over use of NK labor |url=https://www.koreaherald.com/view.php?ud=20170803000597 |access-date=2022-12-06 |website=[[The Korea Herald]] |publisher=[[Yonhap]] |language=en |archive-date=6 December 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221206193602/https://www.koreaherald.com/view.php?ud=20170803000597 |url-status=live}}</ref>

[[Racism in Russia|Racism]] and [[Neo-nazism in Russia|Neo-nazi]] symbols displayed in the past by some Russian football fans drew criticism,<ref>{{Cite news |date=2015-02-27 |title=Study finds pervasive racism in Russian soccer ahead of 2018 World Cup |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/news/early-lead/wp/2015/02/27/study-finds-pervasive-racism-in-russian-soccer-ahead-of-2018-world-cup/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151106104828/https://www.washingtonpost.com/news/early-lead/wp/2015/02/27/study-finds-pervasive-racism-in-russian-soccer-ahead-of-2018-world-cup/ |archive-date=2015-11-06}}</ref> with documented incidents of racial chants, banners spewing hate-filled messages, and sometimes assaults on people from the [[Caucasus]] and [[Central Asia]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Alec Luhn |date=2015-06-04 |title=Racism in Russia laid bare: more than 100 incidents in just two seasons |url=http://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2015/jun/04/racism-in-russia-incidents-world-cup-hosts |url-status=live |archive-url=https://archive.today/20221207182315/https://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2015/jun/04/racism-in-russia-incidents-world-cup-hosts%23comments |archive-date=2022-12-07 |website=[[The Guardian]] |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Tony Wesolowsky |date=August 6, 2015 |title=Ahead Of World Cup, Racism Still Kicking In Russian Soccer |url=https://www.rferl.org/a/russia-soccer-world-cup-racism-still-kicking/27174221.html |website=[[Radio Free Europe/Radio Liberty]] |language=en |access-date=8 December 2022 |archive-date=8 December 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221208225459/https://www.rferl.org/a/russia-soccer-world-cup-racism-still-kicking/27174221.html |url-status=live}}</ref> In March 2015, FIFA's then Vice-President [[Jeffrey Webb (football executive)|Jeffrey Webb]] said that Russia posed a huge challenge from a racism standpoint, and that a World Cup could not be held there under the current conditions.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Olivia Becker |date=March 14, 2015 |title=FIFA Questions 2018 Russian World Cup Due To Racism |url=https://www.vice.com/en/article/fifa-questions-2018-russian-world-cup-due-to-racism/ |website=[[Vice.com]] |language=en |access-date=8 December 2022 |archive-date=8 December 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221208230958/https://www.vice.com/en/article/3ke3ej/fifa-questions-2018-russian-world-cup-due-to-racism |url-status=live}}</ref> On July, [[United Nations]] anti-discrimination official Yuri Boychenko said that Russian soccer authorities had failed to fully grasp what racism was and needed to do more to combat it.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2015-07-23 |title=Russian Authorities Fail to Understand Racism — U.N Official |url=https://www.themoscowtimes.com/2015/07/23/russian-authorities-fail-to-understand-racism-un-official-a48477 |access-date=2022-12-08 |website=[[The Moscow Times]] |language=en |archive-date=8 December 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221208225457/https://www.themoscowtimes.com/2015/07/23/russian-authorities-fail-to-understand-racism-un-official-a48477 |url-status=live}}</ref> To address this as well as concerns of [[hooliganism]] in general, Russian intelligence services blacklisted over 400 fans from entering the stadiums by June 2018, with 32 other countries also sending officers to help local police screen attendees for valid ID cards.<ref>{{cite AV media |date=14 June 2018 |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Wv5N6Z7GYa8 |title=World Cup 2018: Can Russia prevent hooliganism, racism & clashes as it happened during the Euro2016? |publisher=FRANCE 24 English |via=[[YouTube]] |access-date=20 December 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221208225512/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Wv5N6Z7GYa8 |archive-date=8 December 2022 |url-status=live}}</ref>

Allegations of [[corruption]] in the bidding processes and concerns over bribery on the part of the Russian team and corruption by FIFA members for the 2018 and 2022 World Cups led to threats from England's [[The Football Association|FA]] to boycott the tournament.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Ornstein |first=David |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/30077311 |title=World Cup: Former FA chief David Bernstein calls for boycott |date=17 November 2014 |publisher=BBC Sport |access-date=17 November 2014 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141117170857/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/30077311 |archive-date=17 November 2014}}</ref> They claimed that four members of the executive committee had requested bribes to vote for England, and Sepp Blatter had said it had already been arranged before the vote that Russia would win.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.vice.com/en/article/what-we-know-about-corruption-in-the-2018-and-2022-world-cup-bids/ |title=What We Know About Corruption in the 2018 And 2022 World Cup Bids |last=Gordon |first=Aaron |date=27 June 2017 |publisher=vice.com |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180115001654/https://sports.vice.com/en_ca/article/3kzxzv/what-we-know-about-corruption-in-the-2018-and-2022-world-cup-bids |archive-date=15 January 2018 |access-date=31 May 2018}}</ref> FIFA appointed [[Michael J. Garcia]], a US attorney, to investigate and produce [[Garcia Report|a report]] on the corruption allegations. Although the report was never published, FIFA released a 42-page summary of its findings as determined by [[Germans|German]] [[judge]] [[Hans-Joachim Eckert]]. Eckert's summary cleared Russia and Qatar of any wrongdoing, but was denounced by critics as a whitewash.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Dunbar |first=Graham |url=http://bigstory.ap.org/article/4de5fea12fc44b86bd7221bb429f360f/fifa-clears-russia-and-qatar-host-world-cup |title=FIFA under fire after report on Qatar, Russia |access-date=14 November 2014 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141116023958/http://bigstory.ap.org/article/4de5fea12fc44b86bd7221bb429f360f/fifa-clears-russia-and-qatar-host-world-cup |archive-date=16 November 2014 |agency=[[Associated Press]]}}</ref> Because of the controversy, the FA refused to accept Eckert's absolving Russia from blame. [[Greg Dyke]] called for a re-examination of the affair and [[David Bernstein (executive)|David Bernstein]] called for a boycott of the World Cup.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/nov/17/david-bernstein-fa-world-cup-2018-2022-fifa-uefa-boycott |title=Former FA chairman David Bernstein calls for 2018 World Cup boycott |date=17 November 2014 |website=The Guardian |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180426011958/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/nov/17/david-bernstein-fa-world-cup-2018-2022-fifa-uefa-boycott |archive-date=26 April 2018 |access-date=31 May 2018}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-soccer-fifa-qatar/qatar-world-cup-organizers-ready-to-fight-for-2022-idUSKBN0OJ06C20150603 |title=Qatar World Cup organizers ready to fight for 2022 |date=3 June 2015 |access-date=31 May 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180426013532/https://www.reuters.com/article/us-soccer-fifa-qatar/qatar-world-cup-organizers-ready-to-fight-for-2022-idUSKBN0OJ06C20150603 |archive-date=26 April 2018 |work=Reuters}}</ref> Garcia criticised the summary as being "materially incomplete" with "erroneous representations of the facts and conclusions", and appealed to FIFA's Appeal Committee.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Roan |first=Dan |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/30088006 |title=Greg Dyke: FA demands full report findings into World Cup corruption |date=17 November 2014 |publisher=BBC Sport |access-date=17 November 2014 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141117232328/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/30088006 |archive-date=17 November 2014}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Conway |first=Richard |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/0/football/30042309 |title=FIFA corruption report: Who is to blame and what happens now? |publisher=BBC Sport |access-date=14 November 2014 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141114221017/http://www.bbc.com/sport/0/football/30042309 |archive-date=14 November 2014}}</ref> The committee declined to hear his appeal, so Garcia resigned to protest of FIFA's conduct, citing a "lack of leadership" and lack of confidence in Eckert's independence.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/0/football/30522170 |title=Michael Garcia: FIFA investigator resigns in World Cup report row |date=17 December 2014 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160115222149/http://www.bbc.com/sport/0/football/30522170 |archive-date=15 January 2016 |publisher=BBC}}</ref>

On 3 June 2015, the [[Federal Bureau of Investigation|FBI]] confirmed that federal authorities were investigating the bidding and awarding processes for the 2018 and [[2022 FIFA World Cup|2022 World Cups]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/fifa/11650428/Fifa-in-crisis-FBI-extends-investigation-to-Russia-2018-and-Qatar-2022-World-Cup-bids.html |title=Fifa in crisis: FBI extends investigation to Russia 2018 and Qatar 2022 World Cup bids |last=Rumsby |first=Ben |date=4 June 2015 |website=The Telegraph |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150604021845/http://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/fifa/11650428/Fifa-in-crisis-FBI-extends-investigation-to-Russia-2018-and-Qatar-2022-World-Cup-bids.html |archive-date=4 June 2015 |access-date=4 June 2015}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2015/jun/03/fbi-investigating-fifa-2018-2022-world-cup |title=FBI investigating Fifa's awarding of 2018 and 2022 World Cups – report |last=Riach |first=James |date=3 June 2015 |website=The Guardian |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150604102346/http://www.theguardian.com/football/2015/jun/03/fbi-investigating-fifa-2018-2022-world-cup |archive-date=4 June 2015 |access-date=4 June 2015}}</ref> In an interview published on 7 June 2015, [[Domenico Scala (manager)|Domenico Scala]], the head of FIFA's Audit And Compliance Committee, stated that "should there be evidence that the awards to Qatar and Russia came only because of bought votes, then the awards could be cancelled".<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/33041467 |title='Russia & Qatar may lose World Cups' – Fifa official |date=7 June 2015 |publisher=BBC News |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150802010929/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/33041467 |archive-date=2 August 2015}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2015/jun/07/russia-qatar-lose-world-cups-if-bribery-found-fifa |title=Russia and Qatar may lose World Cups if evidence of bribery is found |last=Gibson |first=Owen |date=7 June 2015 |website=The Guardian |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161226061720/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2015/jun/07/russia-qatar-lose-world-cups-if-bribery-found-fifa |archive-date=26 December 2016}}</ref> [[William, Prince of Wales|Prince William of Wales]] and former [[Prime Minister of the United Kingdom|British Prime Minister]] [[David Cameron]] attended a meeting with FIFA vice-president [[Chung Mong-joon]] in which a vote-trading deal for the right to host the 2018 World Cup in [[England 2018 FIFA World Cup bid|England]] was discussed.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/2017/06/27/prince-william-david-cameron-caught-fifa-corruption-scandal/ |title=Prince William and David Cameron caught up in Fifa corruption scandal |last1=Mendick |first1=Robert |last2=Rumsby |first2=Ben |date=27 June 2017 |website=The Daily Telegraph |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180120140450/http://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/2017/06/27/prince-william-david-cameron-caught-fifa-corruption-scandal/ |archive-date=20 January 2018}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.belfasttelegraph.co.uk/video-news/video-david-cameron-and-prince-william-implicated-in-fifa-corruption-probe-35874189.html |title=Video: David Cameron and Prince William implicated in FIFA corruption probe |date=28 June 2017 |website=[[The Belfast Telegraph]] |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190105180723/https://www.belfasttelegraph.co.uk/video-news/video-david-cameron-and-prince-william-implicated-in-fifa-corruption-probe-35874189.html |archive-date=5 January 2019 |access-date=20 May 2018}}</ref>

==Teams==
===Qualification===
{{Main|2018 FIFA World Cup qualification}}

For the first time in the history of the FIFA World Cup, all eligible nations—the 209 [[List of FIFA Member Associations|FIFA member associations]] except automatically qualified hosts Russia—applied to enter the qualifying process.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/road-to-russia-with-new-milestone-2507253 |title=Road to Russia with new milestone |date=15 January 2015 |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150321054553/http://www.fifa.com/worldcup/russia2018/news/newsid=2507253/index.html |archive-date=21 March 2015}}</ref> [[Zimbabwe national football team|Zimbabwe]] and [[Indonesia national football team|Indonesia]] were later disqualified before playing their first matches,<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/zimbabwe-expelled-from-the-preliminary-competition-of-the-2018-fifa-wo-2557911 |title=Zimbabwe expelled from the preliminary competition of the 2018 FIFA World Cup Russia |date=12 March 2015 |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171116134259/https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/y=2015/m=3/news=zimbabwe-expelled-from-the-preliminary-competition-of-the-2018-fifa-wo-2557911.html/index.html |archive-date=16 November 2017}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |url=https://uk.reuters.com/article/uk-soccer-world-indonesia/indonesia-excluded-from-2018-world-cup-qualifiers-idUKKBN0OJ11420150603 |title=Indonesia excluded from 2018 World Cup qualifiers |date=3 June 2015 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160301142102/http://www.the-afc.com/media-releases/impact-of-football-association-of-indonesia-suspension |archive-date=1 March 2016 |work=[[Reuters]]}}</ref> while [[Gibraltar national football team|Gibraltar]] and [[Kosovo national football team|Kosovo]], who joined FIFA on 13 May 2016 after the qualifying draw but before European qualifying had begun, also entered the competition.{{citation needed|date=October 2020}} Places in the tournament were allocated to continental confederations, with the allocation unchanged from the 2014 World Cup.<ref name="fifa20150320">{{Cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/qatar2022/news/y=2015/m=3/news=2022-fifa-world-cup-to-be-played-in-november-december-2568172.html |title=2022 FIFA World Cup to be played in November/December |date=20 March 2015 |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171112185227/https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/qatar2022/news/y=2015/m=3/news=2022-fifa-world-cup-to-be-played-in-november-december-2568172.html |archive-date=12 November 2017}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/y=2015/m=5/news=current-allocation-of-fifa-world-cuptm-confederation-slots-maintained-2610611.html |title=Current allocation of FIFA World Cup confederation slots maintained |date=30 May 2015 |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171116131907/https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/y=2015/m=5/news=current-allocation-of-fifa-world-cuptm-confederation-slots-maintained-2610611.html |archive-date=16 November 2017}}</ref> The first qualification game, between [[East Timor national football team|Timor-Leste]] and [[Mongolia national football team|Mongolia]], began in [[Dili]] on 12 March 2015 as part of the [[2018 FIFA World Cup qualification (AFC)|AFC's qualification]],<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/y=2015/m=3/news=long-road-to-russia-begins-in-dili-2557346.html |title=Long road to Russia begins in Dili |date=11 March 2015 |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171116134405/https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/y=2015/m=3/news=long-road-to-russia-begins-in-dili-2557346.html |archive-date=16 November 2017}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/y=2015/m=7/news=fifa-world-cuptm-preliminary-draw-1-week-to-go-2665858.html |title=FIFA World Cup Preliminary Draw: 1 week to go |date=18 July 2015 |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171116133826/https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/y=2015/m=7/news=fifa-world-cuptm-preliminary-draw-1-week-to-go-2665858.html |archive-date=16 November 2017}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/russia2018/organisation/media/newsid=2035763/index.html |title=Organising Committee for the FIFA World Cup extends its responsibilities to cover 2018 and 2022 |date=19 March 2013 |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141018005611/http://www.fifa.com/worldcup/russia2018/organisation/media/newsid=2035763/index.html |archive-date=18 October 2014}}</ref> and the main qualifying draw took place at the [[Konstantinovsky Palace]] in [[Strelna]], Saint Petersburg, on 25 July 2015.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/russia2018/news/newsid=2455314/index.html |title=Konstantinovsky Palace to stage Preliminary Draw of the 2018 FIFA World Cup |date=10 October 2014 |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141231062541/http://www.fifa.com/worldcup/russia2018/news/newsid=2455314/index.html |archive-date=31 December 2014}}</ref><ref>{{Cite press release |title=Ethics: Executive Committee unanimously supports recommendation to publish report on 2018/2022 FIFA World Cup bidding process |date=19 December 2014 |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |url=https://www.fifa.com/aboutfifa/organisation/news/newsid=2494723/index.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150329105006/http://www.fifa.com/aboutfifa/organisation/news/newsid=2494723/index.html |archive-date=29 March 2015}}</ref>

Of the 32 nations qualified to play at the 2018 FIFA World Cup, 20 countries competed at the previous tournament in [[2014 FIFA World Cup|2014]]. Both Iceland and Panama qualified for the first time, with the former becoming the [[List of countries and dependencies by population|smallest country in terms of population]] to reach the World Cup.<ref>{{Cite news |last=T.A.W. |url=https://www.economist.com/blogs/gametheory/2017/11/dark-norses |title=How Iceland (population: 330,000) qualified for the World Cup |date=12 November 2017 |newspaper=[[The Economist]] |access-date=13 November 2017 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171113051656/https://www.economist.com/blogs/gametheory/2017/11/dark-norses |archive-date=13 November 2017}}</ref>

