===Video assistant referees===
Shortly after the [[International Football Association Board]]'s decision to incorporate [[video assistant referee]]s (VARs) into the [[Laws of the Game (association football)|Laws of the game]] (LOTG) on 16 March 2018, the [[FIFA Council]] took the much-anticipated step of approving the use of VAR for the first time in a FIFA World Cup tournament.<ref name="fifa_20180316">{{Cite web |url=https://fifa.com/about-fifa/news/y=2018/m=3/news=fifa-council-decides-on-key-steps-for-the-future-of-international-competitions.html |title=FIFA Council decides on key steps for the future of international competitions |date=16 March 2018 |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180331053615/http://www.fifa.com/about-fifa/news/y=2018/m=3/news=fifa-council-decides-on-key-steps-for-the-future-of-international-competitions.html |archive-date=31 March 2018}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/43269186 |title=VAR: Video assistant referees set to be used at 2018 World Cup in Russia |date=3 March 2018 |publisher=BBC Sport |access-date=27 June 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180618052157/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/43269186 |archive-date=18 June 2018}}</ref>

VAR operations for all games were operated from a single headquarters in Moscow, which received live video of the games and were in radio contact with the on-field referees.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2018/jun/12/how-will-var-work-at-the-world-cup-and-how-much-is-riding-on-it |title=How will VAR work at the World Cup and how much is riding on it? |last=MacInnes |first=Paul |date=12 June 2018 |website=[[The Guardian]] |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180618073414/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2018/jun/12/how-will-var-work-at-the-world-cup-and-how-much-is-riding-on-it |archive-date=18 June 2018 |access-date=27 June 2018}}</ref> Systems were in place for communicating VAR-related information to broadcasters and visuals on stadiums' large screens were used for the fans in attendance.<ref name=":0" />

VAR had a significant impact on several games.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2018/jun/22/var-at-the-world-cup-the-big-decisions-game-by-game |title=VAR at the World Cup: the big decisions, game by game |last=Belam |first=Martin |date=22 June 2018 |website=The Guardian |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180628015511/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2018/jun/22/var-at-the-world-cup-the-big-decisions-game-by-game |archive-date=28 June 2018 |access-date=27 June 2018}}</ref> On 15 June 2018, [[Diego Costa]]'s first goal against Portugal became the first World Cup goal based on a VAR decision;<ref>{{Cite news |last=Austin |first=Jack |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/world-cup/world-cup-2018-var-diego-costa-spain-portugal-goal-video-assistant-referee-a8401406.html |title=Why Diego Costa's goal against Portugal made World Cup history |date=15 June 2018 |access-date=16 June 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180616131925/https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/world-cup/world-cup-2018-var-diego-costa-spain-portugal-goal-video-assistant-referee-a8401406.html |archive-date=16 June 2018 |website=[[The Independent]]}}</ref> the first penalty as a result of a VAR decision was awarded to France in their match against Australia on 16 June and resulted in a goal by [[Antoine Griezmann]].<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/world-cup/world-cup-2018-var-penalty-decision-france-vs-australia-antoine-griezmann-video-a8402071.html |title=World Cup 2018: History made with first penalty awarded by VAR during France vs Australia |date=16 June 2018 |work=The Independent |access-date=18 June 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180616163535/https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/world-cup/world-cup-2018-var-penalty-decision-france-vs-australia-antoine-griezmann-video-a8402071.html |archive-date=16 June 2018}}</ref> A record number of penalties were awarded in the tournament, a phenomenon partially attributed to VAR.<ref>{{Cite news |url=http://www.espn.co.uk/football/fifa-world-cup/story/3544013/world-cup-breaks-penalty-kick-record-with-help-of-video-assistant-referees |title=Ronaldo penalty sets new World Cup record |date=26 June 2018 |access-date=27 June 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180627202721/http://www.espn.co.uk/football/fifa-world-cup/story/3544013/world-cup-breaks-penalty-kick-record-with-help-of-video-assistant-referees |archive-date=27 June 2018 |publisher=[[ESPN]]}}</ref> Overall, the new technology was both praised and criticised by commentators.<ref>{{Cite news |last=McLean |first=Max |url=https://www.independent.ie/world-news/and-finally/12-very-different-opinions-on-var-at-the-world-cup-to-help-you-make-your-mind-up-37017583.html |title=12 very different opinions on VAR at the World Cup to help you make your mind up |date=16 June 2018 |access-date=27 June 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180627202837/https://www.independent.ie/world-news/and-finally/12-very-different-opinions-on-var-at-the-world-cup-to-help-you-make-your-mind-up-37017583.html |archive-date=27 June 2018 |website=[[The Irish Independent]]}}</ref> FIFA declared the implementation of VAR a success after the first week of competition.<ref>{{Cite news |url=http://www.espn.co.uk/football/fifa-world-cup/story/3535547/fifa-extremely-satisfied-with-world-cup-refereeing-and-calls-var-a-success |title=FIFA 'satisfied' with World Cup refereeing, VAR |date=20 June 2018 |access-date=27 June 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180627202432/http://www.espn.co.uk/football/fifa-world-cup/story/3535547/fifa-extremely-satisfied-with-world-cup-refereeing-and-calls-var-a-success |archive-date=27 June 2018 |publisher=ESPN}}</ref>

==Venues==
[[File:EstadioCentral2017-01-26.jpg|thumb|Reconstruction of the Yekaterinburg Central Stadium in January 2017]]
Russia proposed the following host cities: [[Kaliningrad]], [[Kazan]], [[Krasnodar]], [[Moscow]], [[Nizhny Novgorod]], [[Rostov-on-Don]], [[Saint Petersburg]], [[Samara]], [[Saransk]], [[Sochi]], [[Volgograd]], [[Yaroslavl]], and [[Yekaterinburg]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/mm/document/tournament/competition/01/33/74/52/b5ruse.pdf |title=2018 FIFA World Cup Bid Evaluation Report: Russia |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20101214044951/http://www.fifa.com/mm/document/tournament/competition/01/33/74/52/b5ruse.pdf |archive-date=14 December 2010 |access-date=10 December 2002}}</ref> To reduce travel time for the teams in the huge country, each chosen city was located in or close to [[European Russia]] (those geographically in [[Asia]] being Yekaterinburg<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://welcome2018.com/en/places/ekaterinburg/2561411/ |title=The border between Europe and Asia |website=welcome2018.com |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180623113303/http://welcome2018.com/en/places/ekaterinburg/2561411/ |archive-date=23 June 2018 |access-date=23 June 2018}}</ref> and, according to some definitions for [[Boundaries between the continents|the border]], Sochi). The bid evaluation report stated: "The Russian bid proposes 13 host cities and 16 stadiums, thus exceeding FIFA's minimum requirement. Three of the 16 stadiums would be renovated, and 13 would be newly constructed."<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.dailynews.lk/?q=sports/russia-host-next-fifa-world-cup |title=Russia to host next FIFA World Cup |last=Marikar |first=Hafiz |website=Dailynews.lk |publisher=The Associated Newspapers of Ceylon Ltd |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140809164003/http://www.dailynews.lk/?q=sports%2Frussia-host-next-fifa-world-cup |archive-date=9 August 2014 |access-date=23 July 2014}}</ref>

In October 2011, Russia reduced the number of stadiums from 16 to 14. Construction of the proposed Podolsk stadium in the [[Moscow Oblast]] was cancelled by the regional government. Also, in the capital, [[Lukoil Arena]] was competing with [[VTB Arena]] over which would be constructed first.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.izvestia.ru/news/503276 |title=Почему у России осталось только 14 стадионов к ЧМ-2018 – Известия (Why are there only 14 stadiums for the Russia 2018 World Cup) |date=7 October 2011 |website=Izvestia |language=ru |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111008034435/http://www.izvestia.ru/news/503276 |archive-date=8 October 2011 |access-date=8 October 2011}}</ref>

The final choice of host cities was announced on 29 September 2012. The number of cities was reduced further to 11 and the number of stadiums to 12 as Krasnodar and Yaroslavl were dropped from the final list. Of the 12 stadiums used for the tournament, three ([[Luzhniki Stadium|Luzhniki]], [[Central Stadium (Yekaterinburg)|Yekaterinburg]] and [[Fisht Olympic Stadium|Sochi]]) had been extensively renovated and the other nine were brand new; $11.8&nbsp;billion was spent on hosting the tournament.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/russia2018/organisation/media/newsid=1711717/index.html?intcmp=fifacom_hp_module_media_statements |title=2018 FIFA World Cup to be played in 11 Host Cities |date=29 September 2012 |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140708201259/http://www.fifa.com/worldcup/russia2018/organisation/media/newsid=1711717/index.html?intcmp=fifacom_hp_module_media_statements |archive-date=8 July 2014 |access-date=29 September 2012}}</ref>

Sepp Blatter had said in July 2014 that, given the concerns over the completion of venues in Russia, the number of venues for the tournament may be reduced from 12 to 10.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/russia2018/news/newsid=1711839/index.html |title=Russia united for 2018 FIFA World Cup Host Cities announcement |date=29 September 2012 |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131113044933/http://www.fifa.com/worldcup/russia2018/news/newsid=1711839/index.html |archive-date=13 November 2013 |access-date=13 November 2013}}</ref> He also said, "We are not going to be in a situation, as is the case of one, two or even three stadiums in [[2010 FIFA World Cup]], where it is a problem of what you do with these stadiums".<ref>{{Cite news |last=Saakov |first=Rafael |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/world-europe-28409784 |title=Russia 2018: Major challenges for next World Cup hosts |date=22 July 2014 |access-date=29 July 2014 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140728161154/http://www.bbc.co.uk/news/world-europe-28409784 |archive-date=28 July 2014 |publisher=BBC News}}</ref>

In October 2014, on their first official visit to Russia, FIFA's inspection committee and its head, [[Chris Unger]], visited St. Petersburg, Sochi, Kazan and both Moscow venues. They were satisfied with the progress.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Rogovitskiy |first=Dmitriy |url=http://uk.reuters.com/article/uk-soccer-world-russia-inspection-idUKKCN0IA23420141021 |title=Russia on track for World Cup 2018, say FIFA inspectors |date=21 October 2014 |access-date=29 October 2014 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141029132143/http://uk.reuters.com/article/2014/10/21/uk-soccer-world-russia-inspection-idUKKCN0IA23420141021 |archive-date=29 October 2014 |work=Reuters}}</ref> On 8 October 2015, FIFA and the local organising committee agreed on the official names of the stadiums to be used during the tournament.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/y=2015/m=10/news=stadium-names-for-the-2018-fifa-world-cup-russiatm-confirmed-2709881.html |title=Stadium names for the 2018 FIFA World Cup Russia confirmed |date=8 October 2015 |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171111152009/https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/y=2015/m=10/news=stadium-names-for-the-2018-fifa-world-cup-russiatm-confirmed-2709881.html |archive-date=11 November 2017}}</ref> Of the twelve venues, the Luzhniki Stadium in Moscow and the Saint Petersburg Stadium—the two largest stadiums in Russia—were used most; both hosted seven matches. Sochi, Kazan, Nizhny Novgorod and Samara each hosted six matches, including one quarter-final match each, while the [[Lukoil Arena|Otkritie Stadium]] in Moscow and the [[Rostov Arena|Rostov Stadium]] hosted five matches, including one round-of-16 match each. [[Volgograd Arena]], [[Kaliningrad Stadium]], [[Central Stadium (Yekaterinburg)]] and [[Mordovia Arena]] each hosted four matches, but did not host any knockout stage games.

