==Preparation and costs==
===Budget===
[[File:Scale model of Volgograd Arena.jpg|thumb|Scale model of the [[Volgograd Arena]] whose construction began in 2015.]]
At an estimated cost of over $14.2&nbsp;billion {{as of|2018|June|lc=y}},<ref name="rbc">{{Cite web |url=https://www.rbc.ru/society/08/06/2018/5b02f8039a7947289e44a869 |title=Непредвиденные расходы: как менялась смета ЧМ-2018 |date=8 June 2018 |website=rbc.ru |language=ru |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180613035613/https://www.rbc.ru/society/08/06/2018/5b02f8039a7947289e44a869 |archive-date=13 June 2018 |access-date=16 June 2018}}</ref> the 2018 FIFA event was the most expensive World Cup in history, surpassing the $11.6&nbsp;billion cost of the [[2014 FIFA World Cup]] in Brazil.<ref name="rbc" /><ref name="risen">{{Cite news |url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/sports/soccer/2017/10/24/russias-2018-world-cup-costs-grow-by-600m/106953076/ |title=Russia's 2018 World Cup costs grow by $600&nbsp;million |date=24 October 2017 |work=[[USA Today]] |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180501232927/https://www.usatoday.com/story/sports/soccer/2017/10/24/russias-2018-world-cup-costs-grow-by-600m/106953076/ |archive-date=1 May 2018}}</ref>

The [[Government of Russia]] had originally earmarked a [[Federal budget of Russia|budget]] of around $20&nbsp;billion,<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://sports.ndtv.com/football/russian-prime-minister-dmitry-medvedev-says-2018-fifa-world-cup-to-cost-20-billion-dollars-1535213 |title=Russian Prime Minister Dmitry Medvedev says 2018 FIFA World Cup to cost 20&nbsp;billion dollars |date=13 June 2013 |publisher=[[NDTV]] |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180302163901/https://sports.ndtv.com/football/russian-prime-minister-dmitry-medvedev-says-2018-fifa-world-cup-to-cost-20-billion-dollars-1535213 |archive-date=2 March 2018}}</ref> which was later slashed to $10&nbsp;billion, for World Cup preparations. Half was spent on transportation infrastructure.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.rbth.com/sport/2016/01/07/2018-world-cup-russia-to-spend-5-billion-on-transport-despite-cuts_555759 |title=2018 World Cup: Russia to spend $5&nbsp;billion on transport, despite cuts |date=7 January 2016 |work=[[Russia Beyond]] |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180302103954/https://www.rbth.com/sport/2016/01/07/2018-world-cup-russia-to-spend-5-billion-on-transport-despite-cuts_555759 |archive-date=2 March 2018}}</ref> As part of the program to prepare for the 2018 FIFA World Cup, a federal sub-program—"Construction and Renovation of Transport Infrastructure"—was implemented with a total budget of ₽352.5&nbsp;billion ([[Ruble|rubles]]), with ₽170.3&nbsp;billion coming from the federal budget, ₽35.1&nbsp;billion from regional budgets, and ₽147.1&nbsp;billion from investors.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.championat.com/business/news-2249017-bjudzhet-chempionata-mira-2018-v-rajone-1-mlrd.html |title=Бюджет чемпионата мира 2018 года – около €1 млрд |date=18 September 2015 |website=Чемпионат |language=ru |trans-title=The budget for the 2018 World Cup is about €1&nbsp;billion |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160105025159/http://www.championat.com/business/news-2249017-bjudzhet-chempionata-mira-2018-v-rajone-1-mlrd.html |archive-date=5 January 2016 |access-date=31 May 2018}}</ref> The biggest item of federal spending was the aviation infrastructure costing ₽117.8&nbsp;billion.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://sport24.ru/news/football/2018-05-03-na-podgotovku-transportnoy-infrastruktury-chm-2018-v-rossii-potracheno-228-mlrd |title=На подготовку транспортной инфраструктуры ЧМ-2018 в России потрачено 228 млрд |date=3 May 2018 |website=sport24.ru |language=ru |trans-title=228&nbsp;billion spent on preparation of the transport infrastructure of the World Cup 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180601111503/https://sport24.ru/news/football/2018-05-03-na-podgotovku-transportnoy-infrastruktury-chm-2018-v-rossii-potracheno-228-mlrd |archive-date=1 June 2018 |access-date=31 May 2018}}</ref> Construction of new hotels was a crucial area of infrastructure development in World Cup host cities. Costs continued to mount as preparations were underway.<ref name="risen" />

===Infrastructure spending===
[[Platov International Airport]] in [[Rostov-on-Don]] was upgraded with automated [[air traffic control]] systems. Modern surveillance, navigation, communication, control, and [[Meteorology|meteorological]] support systems were also installed.<ref name="gazeta">{{Cite web |url=https://www.gazeta.ru/army/2018/03/30/11701340.shtml |title=Алмазная радиосвязь |date=30 March 2018 |website=Газета.Ru |language=ru |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180601111504/https://www.gazeta.ru/army/2018/03/30/11701340.shtml |archive-date=1 June 2018 |access-date=31 May 2018}}</ref> [[Koltsovo International Airport]] in [[Yekaterinburg]] was upgraded with radio-engineering tools for flight operation and received a second runway. [[Saransk Airport]] received a new navigation system; two new hotels were constructed in the city—the Mercure Saransk Centre ([[Accor|Accor Hotels]]) and [[Four Points by Sheraton]] Saransk as well as few other smaller accommodation facilities.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://info-rm.com/2018/01/16/v_nordovii_k_chn2018_proshli_klassifikaciyu_dva_hostela_i_motel.html/ |title=В Мордовии к ЧМ-2018 прошли классификацию два хостела и мотель |date=16 January 2018 |website=info-rm.com |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180314192928/http://info-rm.com/2018/01/16/v_nordovii_k_chn2018_proshli_klassifikaciyu_dva_hostela_i_motel.html/ |archive-date=14 March 2018}}</ref> In [[Samara]], new tram lines were laid.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.samru.ru/society/novosti_samara/104707.html |title=Трамвайная линия до стадиона для ЧМ в Самаре готова на 89% |date=22 February 2018 |website=samru.ru |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180302103956/http://www.samru.ru/society/novosti_samara/104707.html |archive-date=2 March 2018}}</ref> [[Khrabrovo Airport]] in [[Kaliningrad]] was upgraded with radio navigation and weather equipment.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.rusaviainsider.com/kaliningrad-airports-new-terminal-put-operation/ |title=Kaliningrad airport's new terminal put in operation |date=26 July 2017 |publisher=Russian Aviation Insider |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180522214606/http://www.rusaviainsider.com/kaliningrad-airports-new-terminal-put-operation/ |archive-date=22 May 2018 |access-date=4 June 2018}}</ref> Renovation and upgraded radio-engineering tools for flight operations was completed in the [[Myachkovo Airport|Moscow]], [[Pulkovo Airport|Saint Petersburg]], [[Volgograd International Airport|Volgograd]], [[Kurumoch International Airport|Samara]], Yekaterinburg, [[Kazan International Airport|Kazan]] and [[Sochi International Airport|Sochi]] airports.<ref name="gazeta" /> On 27 March, the [[Ministry of Construction, Housing and Utilities]] of Russia reported that all communications within its area of responsibility had been commissioned. The last facility commissioned was a waste treatment station in Volgograd. In Yekaterinburg, where four matches were hosted, hosting costs increased to over ₽7.4&nbsp;billion, exceeding the ₽5.6&nbsp;billion rubles originally allocated from the [[Federal budget of Russia|state]] and regional budget.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://regnum.ru/news/2431507.html |title=В Екатеринбурге увеличились расходы на подготовку к проведению ЧМ-2018 |date=14 June 2018 |website=regnum.ru |language=ru |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180616113334/https://regnum.ru/news/2431507.html |archive-date=16 June 2018 |access-date=16 June 2018}}</ref>

===Volunteers===
[[File:Bel-Tun (3).jpg|thumb|Volunteer flag bearers on the field prior to [[Belgium national football team|Belgium]]'s ''(flag depicted)'' group stage match against [[Tunisia national football team|Tunisia]]]]
Volunteer applications to the 2018 Russia Local Organising Committee opened on 1 June 2016. The 2018 FIFA World Cup Russia Volunteer Program received about 177,000 applications,<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://welcome2018.com/journal/materials/volonterskaya-programma-chm-2018-ustanovila-novyy-rekord/ |title=Новый рекорд волонтерской программы ЧМ-2018 |date=17 January 2017 |website=welcome2018.com |language=ru |trans-title=New record of the Volunteer program of the World Cup 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170822033741/http://welcome2018.com/journal/materials/volonterskaya-programma-chm-2018-ustanovila-novyy-rekord/ |archive-date=22 August 2017 |access-date=31 May 2018}}</ref> and engaged a total of 35,000 volunteers.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://ru.fifa.com/worldcup/news/%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%B1%D0%BE%D1%80-%D0%B2%D0%BE%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%BD%D1%82%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B2-%D0%BD%D0%B0-%D1%87%D0%BC-2018-%D1%83%D1%81%D1%82%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B8%D0%BB-%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%B2%D1%8B%D0%B8-%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%BA%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%B4-%D1%87%D0%B5%D0%BC%D0%BF%D0%B8%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%B0%D1%82%D0%BE%D0%B2-%D0%BC%D0%B8%D1%80%D0%B0-2863622 |title=Набор волонтеров на ЧМ-2018 установил новый рекорд Чемпионатов мира |date=17 January 2017 |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |language=ru |trans-title=A set of volunteers at the World Cup 2018 set a new World Cup record |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180601111503/https://ru.fifa.com/worldcup/news/%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%B1%D0%BE%D1%80-%D0%B2%D0%BE%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%BD%D1%82%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B2-%D0%BD%D0%B0-%D1%87%D0%BC-2018-%D1%83%D1%81%D1%82%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B8%D0%BB-%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%B2%D1%8B%D0%B8-%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%BA%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%B4-%D1%87%D0%B5%D0%BC%D0%BF%D0%B8%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%B0%D1%82%D0%BE%D0%B2-%D0%BC%D0%B8%D1%80%D0%B0-2863622 |archive-date=1 June 2018 |access-date=31 May 2018 |url-status=dead }}</ref> They received training at 15 Volunteer Centres of the local organising committee based in 15 universities, and in volunteer centres in the host cities. Preference, especially in key areas, was given to those with knowledge of a foreign language and volunteering experience, but not necessarily to Russian nationals.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://ru.fifa.com/worldcup/organisation/volunteers/faq/ |title=Чемпионат мира по футболу FIFA 2018 в России – Волонтерская программа: F.A.Q. |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |language=ru |trans-title=FIFA 2018 World Cup in Russia – Volunteer program: F.A.Q. |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161010214935/http://ru.fifa.com/worldcup/organisation/volunteers/faq/ |archive-date=10 October 2016 |access-date=31 May 2018}}</ref>

===Transport===
Free [[public transport]] services were offered for ticketholders during the World Cup, including additional trains linking host cities, as well as services such as bus services within them.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2018/mar/27/world-cup-2018-russia-tickets-travel |title=World Cup 2018: what is a Fan ID and do I need one to watch games in Russia? |last=Ames |first=Nick |date=27 March 2018 |website=The Guardian |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180331062047/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2018/mar/27/world-cup-2018-russia-tickets-travel |archive-date=31 March 2018 |access-date=10 June 2018}}</ref><ref name=":2">{{Cite news |last=Wildie |first=Tom |url=http://www.abc.net.au/news/2018-06-08/fans-russia-world-cup-dream-crushed-by-visa-rejection/9850796 |title=Aussie soccer fan's World Cup dream crushed by new Russian visa rejection |date=8 June 2018 |access-date=10 June 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180609224845/http://www.abc.net.au/news/2018-06-08/fans-russia-world-cup-dream-crushed-by-visa-rejection/9850796 |archive-date=9 June 2018 |publisher=[[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]]}}</ref><ref name=":3">{{Cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-44332038 |title=What World Cup fans going to Russia should know |date=6 June 2018 |access-date=10 June 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180610061428/https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-44332038 |archive-date=10 June 2018 |publisher=BBC News}}</ref>

==Schedule==
[[File:До чемпионата мира по футболу 2018 года – 1000 дней 01.jpg|thumb|Launching of the 1,000 days countdown in Moscow]]
The full schedule was announced by FIFA on 24 July 2015 without kick-off times, which were confirmed later.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/y=2015/m=7/news=match-schedules-for-fifa-confederations-cup-2017-and-2018-fifa-world-c-2667193.html |title=Match schedules for FIFA Confederations Cup 2017 and 2018 FIFA World Cup unveiled |date=24 July 2015 |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171005000543/http://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/y=2015/m=7/news=match-schedules-for-fifa-confederations-cup-2017-and-2018-fifa-world-c-2667193.html |archive-date=5 October 2017}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=http://resources.fifa.com/mm/document/tournament/competition/02/66/71/62/2018fwc_matchschedule_30052017_en_neutral.pdf |title=FIFA World Cup Russia 2018 – Match Schedule |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170911162106/http://resources.fifa.com/mm/document/tournament/competition/02/66/71/62/2018fwc_matchschedule_30052017_en_neutral.pdf |archive-date=11 September 2017}}</ref> On 1 December 2017, following the final draw, FIFA adjusted six kick-off times.<ref name="schedule">{{Cite web |url=https://resources.fifa.com/mm/document/tournament/competition/02/66/71/62/2018fwc_matchschedule_20122017_en_neutral.pdf |title=FIFA World Cup Russia 2018 – Match Schedule |date=20 December 2017 |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180624041527/https://resources.fifa.com/mm/document/tournament/competition/02/66/71/62/2018fwc_matchschedule_20122017_en_neutral.pdf |archive-date=24 June 2018 |access-date=20 December 2017}}</ref><ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Morrin |first=Siobhan |url=https://time.com/5310099/world-cup-russia-watch-teams-players/ |title=Your Ultimate Guide to Watching the 2018 World Cup |date=13 June 2018 |access-date=20 June 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180614022218/http://time.com/5310099/world-cup-russia-watch-teams-players/ |archive-date=14 June 2018 |magazine=[[Time (magazine)|Time]]}}</ref>

Russia was placed in position A1 in the group stage and played in the opening match at the Luzhniki Stadium in Moscow on 14 June against [[Saudi Arabia national football team|Saudi Arabia]], the two lowest-ranked teams of the tournament at the time of the final draw.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/fifa-world-ranking/ranking-table/men/rank=279/index.html |title=The FIFA/Coca-Cola World Ranking – October 2017 |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171016225735/http://www.fifa.com/fifa-world-ranking/ranking-table/men/rank=279/index.html |archive-date=16 October 2017 |access-date=16 October 2017}}</ref> The Luzhniki Stadium also hosted the second semi-final on 11 July and the final on 15 July. The [[Krestovsky Stadium]] in Saint Petersburg hosted the first semi-final on 10 July and the match for third place on 14 July.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/world-cup/2018/06/14/world-cup-2018-fixtures-full-schedule-results-match-dates/ |title=World Cup 2018 fixtures: full schedule, results and match dates |date=14 June 2018 |work=The Telegraph |access-date=14 June 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180614070829/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/world-cup/2018/06/14/world-cup-2018-fixtures-full-schedule-results-match-dates/ |archive-date=14 June 2018}}</ref><ref name="fifa20150320" />

==Opening ceremony==
{{Main|2018 FIFA World Cup opening ceremony}}

[[File:Robbie Williams Aida Garifullina 2018 FIFA World Cup.jpg|thumb|right|Soprano [[Aida Garifullina]] and pop singer [[Robbie Williams]] singing "[[Angels (Robbie Williams song)|Angels]]" at the opening ceremony]]
The opening ceremony took place on Thursday, 14 June 2018, at the Luzhniki Stadium in Moscow, preceding the [[2018 FIFA World Cup Group A#Russia vs Saudi Arabia|opening match of the tournament]] between hosts Russia and Saudi Arabia.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/world-cup/2018/06/13/world-cup-2018-opening-ceremony-time-will-start-will-robbie/ |title=World Cup 2018 Opening Ceremony: What time will it start and when will Robbie Williams feature? |date=13 June 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180613164243/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/world-cup/2018/06/13/world-cup-2018-opening-ceremony-time-will-start-will-robbie/ |archive-date=13 June 2018 |access-date=14 June 2018 |newspaper=The Telegraph}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2018/jun/14/robbie-williams-delivers-for-short-sharp-world-cup-opening-ceremony |title=Robbie Williams show at World Cup opening ceremony is too short to ever be dull |date=14 June 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180614194931/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2018/jun/14/robbie-williams-delivers-for-short-sharp-world-cup-opening-ceremony |archive-date=14 June 2018 |access-date=14 June 2018 |newspaper=The Guardian}}</ref>

At the start of the ceremony, Russian president [[Vladimir Putin]] gave a speech, welcoming the countries of the world to Russia and calling football a uniting force.<ref name="BBC 2018">{{Cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/44487420 |title=World Cup 2018: Ronaldo and Robbie Williams star in opening ceremony |date=14 June 2018 |access-date=15 June 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180615140301/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/44487420 |archive-date=15 June 2018 |publisher=BBC Sport}}</ref> Brazilian World Cup-winning striker [[Ronaldo (Brazilian footballer)|Ronaldo]] entered the stadium with a child in a Russia jersey.<ref name="BBC 2018" /> Pop singer [[Robbie Williams]] then sang two of his songs solo before he and Russian [[soprano]] [[Aida Garifullina]] performed a duet.<ref name="BBC 2018" /> Dancers dressed in the flags of the 32 competing teams appeared carrying a sign with the name of each nation.<ref name="BBC 2018" /> At the end of the ceremony Ronaldo reappeared with the official match ball which had returned from the [[International Space Station]] in early June.<ref name="BBC 2018" />

Young participants of the international children's social programme [[Football for Friendship]] from 211 countries and regions took part in the opening ceremony of the FIFA World Cup at the Luzhniki stadium.<ref>{{cite web |title=211 countries and regions take part in the Sixth International Football for Friendship Children's Forum |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/211-countries-and-regions-took-part-in-the-sixth-international-football-for-frie |access-date=15 November 2020 |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |language=en-GB |archive-date=10 April 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190410102651/https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/211-countries-and-regions-took-part-in-the-sixth-international-football-for-frie |url-status=live}}</ref>

==Group stage==
[[File:2018 world cup.png|thumb|upright=1.75|{{col-begin}}{{col-4}}{{legend|#2b42a3|Champion}}{{legend|#34c0be|Runner-up}}
{{col-4}}{{legend|#269c5a|Third place}}{{legend|#81c846|Fourth place}}
{{col-4}}{{legend|#e4e454|Quarter-finals}}{{legend|#ff9f40|Round of 16}}
{{col-4}}{{legend|#b94954|Group stage}} {{col-end}}]]

Competing countries were divided into eight groups of four teams (groups A to H). Teams in each group played one another in a [[round-robin tournament|round-robin]], with the top two teams advancing to the [[#Knockout stage|knockout stage]]. Ten European teams and four South American teams progressed to the knockout stage, together with Japan and Mexico.

For the first time since [[1938 FIFA World Cup|1938]], Germany, the reigning champions, were eliminated in the first round. This was the third consecutive tournament in which the holders were eliminated in the first round, after Italy in [[2010 FIFA World Cup|2010]] and Spain in [[2014 FIFA World Cup|2014]]. No African team progressed to the second round for the first time since [[1982 FIFA World Cup|1982]]. The fair play criteria came into use for the first time when [[Japan national football team|Japan]] qualified over [[Senegal national football team|Senegal]] because the team had received fewer yellow cards. Only one match, [[Denmark national football team|Denmark]] versus [[France national football team|France]], was goalless. Until then there were a record 36 straight games in which at least one goal was scored.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2018/jun/27/world-cup-france-nabil-fekir-argentina-didier-deschamps |title=What does Didier Deschamps want from his France team? If only he knew |last=White |first=Adam |date=27 June 2018 |website=The Guardian |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180628233357/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2018/jun/27/world-cup-france-nabil-fekir-argentina-didier-deschamps |archive-date=28 June 2018 |access-date=28 June 2018}}</ref> All times listed below are [[Time in Russia|local time]].<ref name="schedule" />

