==Marketing==
[[File:Dusha typeface sample2.tiff|thumb|upright|The typeface "Dusha" used for branding]]

===Branding===
The tournament logo was unveiled on 28 October 2014 by cosmonauts at the [[International Space Station]] and then projected onto Moscow's [[Bolshoi Theatre]] during an evening television programme. Russian Sports Minister [[Vitaly Mutko]] said the logo was inspired by "Russia's rich artistic tradition and its history of bold achievement and innovation", and FIFA president Sepp Blatter stated that it reflected the "heart and soul" of the country.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Tetrault-Farber |first=Gabrielle |date=2014-10-29 |title=Russian Cosmonauts Unveil 2018 World Cup Logo |url=https://www.themoscowtimes.com/2014/10/29/russian-cosmonauts-unveil-2018-world-cup-logo-a40881 |access-date=2025-11-09 |website=[[The Moscow Times]] |language=en |archive-date=17 November 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251117200224/https://www.themoscowtimes.com/2014/10/29/russian-cosmonauts-unveil-2018-world-cup-logo-a40881 |url-status=live }}</ref> For branding, Portuguese design agency Brandia Central created materials in 2014, with a typeface called ''Dusha'' – {{Language with name/for|ru|{{Linktext|душа|lang=ru}}|soul}} – designed by Brandia Central and edited by Adotbelow of the DSType Foundry in Portugal.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://type.today/en/journal/fifa_typography |title=Font scandal at FIFA World Cup |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180723003555/https://type.today/en/journal/fifa_typography |archive-date=23 July 2018 |access-date=22 July 2018}}</ref>

===Broadcasting rights===
{{Main|2018 FIFA World Cup broadcasting rights}}

FIFA, through several companies, sold the broadcasting rights for the 2018 FIFA World Cup to various local broadcasters. After having tested the technology at limited matches of the [[2013 FIFA Confederations Cup]],<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/behind-screen/fifa-confederations-cup-testing-4k-565612 |title=FIFA Confederations Cup Testing 4K; Might Lead to World Cup 'Ultra HD' Broadcast |work=[[The Hollywood Reporter]] |access-date=24 November 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181125115403/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/behind-screen/fifa-confederations-cup-testing-4k-565612 |archive-date=25 November 2018}}</ref> and the 2014 FIFA World Cup (via private tests and public viewings in the host city of [[Rio de Janeiro]]),<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.theverge.com/2014/6/5/5782382/why-cant-you-watch-the-world-cup-in-4k |title=4K TV misses its World Cup goal |work=The Verge |access-date=24 November 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181125074050/https://www.theverge.com/2014/6/5/5782382/why-cant-you-watch-the-world-cup-in-4k |archive-date=25 November 2018}}</ref> the 2018 World Cup was the first World Cup in which all matches were produced in [[4K resolution|4K]] [[Ultra-high-definition television|ultra high definition]]. Host Broadcast Services (HBS) stated that at least 75% of the broadcast cut of each match would come from 4K cameras (covering the majority of main angles), with instant replays and some camera angles being converted up from [[1080p]] high definition sources with limited degradation in quality. These broadcasts were made available from selected rightsholders and television providers.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.tvtechnology.com/news/fox-telemundo-offer-a-clearer-view-of-fifa-world-cup-russia |title=Fox, Telemundo Offer a Clearer View of FIFA World Cup Russia |work=TV Technology |access-date=24 November 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181125074011/https://www.tvtechnology.com/news/fox-telemundo-offer-a-clearer-view-of-fifa-world-cup-russia |archive-date=25 November 2018}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/44305845 |title=World Cup 2018: BBC to show tournament in Ultra HD & virtual reality |date=30 May 2018 |publisher=BBC Sport |access-date=24 November 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181125134013/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/44305845 |archive-date=25 November 2018}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |url=http://www.sportspromedia.com/news/fubotv-streaming-world-cup-4k-resolution |title=FuboTV streaming World Cup in 4K resolution |work=[[SportsPro]] |access-date=24 November 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181214024835/http://www.sportspromedia.com/news/fubotv-streaming-world-cup-4k-resolution |archive-date=14 December 2018}}</ref>

In February 2018, [[Ukraine|Ukrainian]] rightsholder [[UA:PBC]] stated that it would not broadcast the World Cup due to existing tensions with Russia amidst the [[Russo-Ukrainian war|Russo-Ukrainian War]]. This came in the wake of growing boycott of the tournament by the [[Ukrainian Association of Football]] and sports minister [[Ihor Zhdanov]].<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.kyivpost.com/sport/ukrainian-public-broadcaster-decides-not-show-world-cup-matches.html |title=Ukrainian public broadcaster decides not to show World Cup matches |date=12 February 2018 |work=[[Kyiv Post]] |access-date=1 March 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180314192928/https://www.kyivpost.com/sport/ukrainian-public-broadcaster-decides-not-show-world-cup-matches.html |archive-date=14 March 2018}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Carp |first=Sam |url=http://www.sportspromedia.com/news/ukraine-broadcaster-refuses-to-show-russia-world-cup |title=Ukraine's public broadcaster refuses to show Fifa World Cup |work=SportsPro |access-date=1 March 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180314192928/http://www.sportspromedia.com/news/ukraine-broadcaster-refuses-to-show-russia-world-cup |archive-date=14 March 2018}}</ref> Additionally, the FFU refused to accredit journalists for the World Cup and waived their quota of tickets.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.sportbusiness.com/sport-news/inter-media-group-secures-world-cup-rights-ukraine |title=Inter Media Group secures World Cup rights in Ukraine |date=31 May 2018 |access-date=1 July 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180702011117/https://www.sportbusiness.com/sport-news/inter-media-group-secures-world-cup-rights-ukraine |archive-date=2 July 2018 |publisher=TV Sports Markets}}</ref> However, the Ukrainian state TV still broadcast the World Cup, and more than 4&nbsp;million Ukrainians watched the opening match.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Bershidsky |first=Leonid |url=https://www.bloomberg.com/view/articles/2018-06-18/you-can-hate-putin-but-love-the-world-cup |title=You Can Hate Putin But Love the World Cup |date=18 June 2018 |work=[[Bloomberg News]] |access-date=25 June 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180619014004/https://www.bloomberg.com/view/articles/2018-06-18/you-can-hate-putin-but-love-the-world-cup |archive-date=19 June 2018}}</ref>

Broadcast rights to the tournament in the [[Middle East]] were hampered by an ongoing [[Qatar diplomatic crisis]], which saw [[Bahrain]], [[Egypt]], [[Saudi Arabia]], and [[United Arab Emirates]] cut diplomatic ties with Qatar—the home country of FIFA's Middle East and Africa rightsholder [[BeIN Sports]]—in June 2017, over its [[Qatar and state-sponsored terrorism|alleged state support of terrorist groups]]. On 2 June 2018, beIN pulled its channels from [[Du (company)|Du]] and [[Etisalat]], but with service to the latter restored later that day. Etisalat subsequently announced that it would air the World Cup in the UAE, and continue to offer beIN normally and without interruptions.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.khaleejtimes.com/nation/dubai/uae-football-fans-fear-missing-out-on-world-cup-action- |title=UAE football fans fear missing out on Fifa World Cup action |last=Gokulan |first=Dhanusha |website=Khaleej Times |date=3 June 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180826214531/https://www.khaleejtimes.com/nation/dubai/uae-football-fans-fear-missing-out-on-world-cup-action- |archive-date=26 August 2018 |access-date=26 August 2018}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.arabianbusiness.com/media/398024-bein-sports-pulls-tv-channels-from-du-customers-in-the-uae |title=beIN Sports pulls TV channels from du customers in the UAE |work=[[Arabian Business|ArabianBusiness.com]] |access-date=26 August 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180826183043/https://www.arabianbusiness.com/media/398024-bein-sports-pulls-tv-channels-from-du-customers-in-the-uae |archive-date=26 August 2018}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.arabianbusiness.com/media/398069-etisalat-announces-fifa-world-cup-2018-packages |title=Etisalat announces FIFA World Cup 2018 package |work=ArabianBusiness.com |access-date=26 August 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180826183045/https://www.arabianbusiness.com/media/398069-etisalat-announces-fifa-world-cup-2018-packages |archive-date=26 August 2018}}</ref> In Saudi Arabia, beIN was banned from doing business; as a result, its channels and other content have been widely and illegally repackaged by a broadcaster identifying itself as "[[BeoutQ]]". While FIFA attempted to negotiate the sale of a package consisting of Saudi matches and the final indirectly, they were unable to do so. On 12 July 2018, FIFA stated that it had "engaged counsel to take legal action in Saudi Arabia and is working alongside other sports rights owners that have also been affected to protect its interests."<ref>{{Cite news |last=Vivarelli |first=Nick |url=https://variety.com/2018/tv/news/world-cup-middle-east-piracy-saudi-arabia-qatar-blockade-1202842354/ |title=World Cup: Diplomatic Crisis in Middle East Stokes Fears of Piracy |date=13 June 2018 |work=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]] |access-date=26 August 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180826113341/https://variety.com/2018/tv/news/world-cup-middle-east-piracy-saudi-arabia-qatar-blockade-1202842354/ |archive-date=26 August 2018}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.arabianbusiness.com/media/400550-fifa-to-take-legal-action-against-beoutq-over-world-cup-broadcasts |title=FIFA to take legal action against beoutQ over World Cup broadcasts |work=Arabian Business |access-date=26 August 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180826183049/https://www.arabianbusiness.com/media/400550-fifa-to-take-legal-action-against-beoutq-over-world-cup-broadcasts |archive-date=26 August 2018}}</ref>

In the United States, the 2018 World Cup was the first men's World Cup whose English rights were held by [[Fox Sports (United States)|Fox Sports]], and Spanish rights held by [[Telemundo Deportes|Telemundo]]. The elimination of the [[United States men's national soccer team|United States]] in the qualifiers led to concerns that US interest and viewership of this World Cup would be reduced, noting that "casual" viewers of US matches caused them to peak at 16.5&nbsp;million in 2014, and determined how much Fox paid for the rights. During a launch event prior to the elimination, Fox stated that it had planned to place a secondary focus on the Mexican team in its coverage to take advantage of their popularity among [[Hispanic and Latino Americans]]. Fox stated that it was still committed to broadcasting a significant amount of tournament coverage.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Draper |first=Kevin |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2017/10/11/sports/soccer/world-cup-soccer.html |title=Fox and Others Will Feel Pain of U.S. Absence at World Cup |date=11 October 2017 |work=The New York Times |access-date=26 October 2017 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171024072730/https://www.nytimes.com/2017/10/11/sports/soccer/world-cup-soccer.html |archive-date=24 October 2017}}</ref><ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Deitsch |first=Richard |url=https://www.si.com/tech-media/2017/10/11/usa-world-cup-elimination-fox-sports-coverage-mexico-el-tri |title=With USMNT eliminated from World Cup, Fox must refocus coverage plan |date=11 October 2017 |magazine=Sports Illustrated |access-date=26 October 2017 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171026111153/https://www.si.com/tech-media/2017/10/11/usa-world-cup-elimination-fox-sports-coverage-mexico-el-tri |archive-date=26 October 2017}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last1=Shaw |first1=Lucas |url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2017-10-11/fox-is-said-to-project-20-million-in-lost-world-cup-ad-sales |title=Fox projects up to $20&nbsp;million in lost World Cup ad sales |date=11 October 2017 |access-date=26 October 2017 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171026111201/https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2017-10-11/fox-is-said-to-project-20-million-in-lost-world-cup-ad-sales |archive-date=26 October 2017 |publisher=Bloomberg |last2=Novy-Williams |first2=Eben |last3=Broudway |first3=Ira}}</ref> Viewership was down overall compared to 2014; match scheduling and time zones were not as favourable to viewers in the Americas as they were in 2014. Many games aired in the morning hours, although Telemundo's broadcast of the Mexico-Sweden Group F match was announced as being its most-watched weekday [[Daytime television|daytime]] program in the network's history.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Steinberg |first=Brian |url=https://variety.com/2018/tv/news/world-cup-ratings-fox-telemundo-tv-viewership-1202864539/ |title=World Cup Strains to Reverse Sports-Viewing Trends |date=3 July 2018 |work=Variety |access-date=14 October 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181014091321/https://variety.com/2018/tv/news/world-cup-ratings-fox-telemundo-tv-viewership-1202864539/ |archive-date=14 October 2018}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Ingold |first=Ira Boudway, Eben Novy-Williams, David |url=http://www.chicagotribune.com/sports/soccer/ct-spt-world-cup-ratings-20180621-story.html |title=Fox and Telemundo 2018 World Cup ratings are down 44 percent from 2014 |work=[[Chicago Tribune]] |access-date=14 October 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181014091343/http://www.chicagotribune.com/sports/soccer/ct-spt-world-cup-ratings-20180621-story.html |archive-date=14 October 2018}}</ref>

Unlike previous tournaments, where the rights were bundled with those of [[South Korea]], [[Korean Central Television]] acquired rights to the 2018 World Cup within [[North Korea]]. Broadcasts only began with the round of 16, and matches were tape delayed and edited for time. In addition, matches involving Japan were excluded from the broadcasts, due to [[Japan–North Korea relations|strained relations and campaigns against the country]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.northkoreatech.org/2018/07/10/north-korean-tv-world-cup/ |title=North Korean TV picks up World Cup from Round of 16 |last=Williams |first=Martyn |date=9 July 2018 |website=North Korea Tech |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190528233345/https://www.northkoreatech.org/2018/07/10/north-korean-tv-world-cup/ |archive-date=28 May 2019 |access-date=29 May 2019}}</ref>

===Ticketing===
The first phase of ticket sales started on 14 September 2017, 12:00 [[Moscow Time]], and lasted until 12 October 2017.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/y=2017/m=9/news=ticket-sales-for-2018-fifa-world-cuptm-to-start-on-14-september-2017-2907645.html |title=Ticket sales for 2018 FIFA World Cup to start on 14 September 2017 |date=12 September 2017 |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171113113813/https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/y=2017/m=9/news=ticket-sales-for-2018-fifa-world-cuptm-to-start-on-14-september-2017-2907645.html |archive-date=13 November 2017}}</ref>

The general [[visa policy of Russia]] did not apply to participants and spectators, who were able to visit Russia without a visa right before and during the competition regardless of their citizenship. Spectators were nonetheless required to register for a "Fan-ID", a special photo identification pass. A Fan-ID was required to enter the country visa-free, while a ticket, Fan-ID and a valid passport were required to enter stadiums for matches. Fan-IDs also granted World Cup attendees free access to public transport services, including buses, and [[Rail transport in Russia|train service]] between host cities. Fan-ID was administered by the [[Minkomsvyaz|Ministry of Digital Development, Communications and Mass Media]], which could revoke this [[accreditation]] at any time to "ensure the defence capability or security of the state or public order".<ref name=":1" /><ref name=":2" /><ref name=":3" />

===Merchandise===
{{See also|FIFA World Cup video games}}
On 29 May 2018, [[Electronic Arts]] released a free update to their video game ''[[FIFA 18]]'' that added content related to the 2018 FIFA World Cup. The [[expansion pack|expansion]] included a World Cup tournament mode with all teams and stadiums from the event, official television presentation elements, and World Cup-related content for the Ultimate Team mode.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.eurogamer.net/articles/2018-04-30-fifa-18-gets-free-world-cup-mode-this-month |title=FIFA 18 gets free World Cup mode in May |last=Yin-Poole |first=Wesley |website=[[Eurogamer]] |date=30 April 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180501225232/https://www.eurogamer.net/articles/2018-04-30-fifa-18-gets-free-world-cup-mode-this-month |archive-date=1 May 2018 |access-date=14 May 2018}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.gamespot.com/articles/fifa-18-world-cup-update-out-now-for-free-heres-wh/1100-6459264/ |title=FIFA 18 World Cup Update Out Now For Free, Here's What It Adds |last=Dayus |first=Oscar |date=30 May 2018 |website=[[GameSpot]] |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180614013530/https://www.gamespot.com/articles/fifa-18-world-cup-update-out-now-for-free-heres-wh/1100-6459264/ |archive-date=14 June 2018 |access-date=14 March 2019}}</ref>

[[Panini Group]] continued their partnership with FIFA by producing stickers for their World Cup [[sticker album]].<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.fifa.com/about-fifa/marketing/licensing/brand-collaborations.html |title=Brand collaborations |access-date=7 October 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180909000336/https://www.fifa.com/about-fifa/marketing/licensing/brand-collaborations.html |archive-date=9 September 2018 |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association}}</ref> Panini also developed an app for the 2018 World Cup where fans could collect and swap virtual stickers, with 5&nbsp;million fans gathering digital stickers for the tournament.<ref>{{Cite magazine |url=https://www.si.com/soccer/2018/06/06/panini-world-cup-sticker-album-history-tradition |title=Panini World Cup sticker album: Inside story behind the craze |magazine=[[Sports Illustrated]] |access-date=7 October 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180812052729/https://www.si.com/soccer/2018/06/06/panini-world-cup-sticker-album-history-tradition |archive-date=12 August 2018}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.coca-colacompany.com/stories/paninis-fifa-world-cup-digital-sticker-album-is-more-popular#ath |title=12 Years Running: Panini's FIFA World Cup Digital Sticker Album is More Popular Than Ever |access-date=7 October 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181005111908/https://www.coca-colacompany.com/stories/paninis-fifa-world-cup-digital-sticker-album-is-more-popular#ath |archive-date=5 October 2018 |publisher=Coca-Cola Company}}</ref>

==Symbols==
===Mascot===
{{Main|Zabivaka}}

[[File:Rus-IvoryCoast (13).jpg|thumb|upright|Tournament mascot, wolf Zabivaka]]
The tournament's [[FIFA World Cup mascot|official mascot]] was unveiled on 21 October 2016, and selected through a design competition among university students. A public vote was used to select the mascot from three finalists—a cat, a tiger, and a wolf. The winner, with 53% or approximately 1&nbsp;million votes, was Zabivaka—an [[Anthropomorphism|anthropomorphic]] wolf dressed in the colours of the Russian national team. Zabivaka's name is a portmanteau of the Russian words [[:wikt:забияка#Russian|забияка]] ("hothead") and [[:wikt:забивать#Russian|забивать]] ("to score"), and his official backstory states that he is an aspiring football player who is "charming, confident and social".<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2016/oct/21/russia-2018-zabivaka-world-cup-mascot |title=Russia 2018: World Cup mascot Zabivaka the wolf unveiled in Moscow |date=21 October 2016 |website=The Guardian |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170325112015/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2016/oct/21/russia-2018-zabivaka-world-cup-mascot |archive-date=25 March 2017 |access-date=10 June 2018}}</ref>

===Match ball===
{{Main|Adidas Telstar 18}}

[[File:Adidas Telstar 18 in Russia vs. Argentina.jpg|thumb|upright|Match ball "Telstar 18"]]
[[File:Adidas Telstar Mechta Ball On the Handover Ceremony of the 2022 FIFA World Cup host mantle.jpg|thumb|upright|Match ball for the knockout stage, "Telstar Mechta"]]
The official match ball, the "[[Adidas Telstar 18|Telstar 18]]", was unveiled on 9 November 2017. It was based on the name and design of the first [[Adidas]] World Cup ball from [[1970 FIFA World Cup|1970]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.skysports.com/football/news/15241/9381511/world-cup-final-was-lionel-messi-really-a-disappointment-in-brazil-or-have-we-just-become-numb-to-his-genius |title=World Cup Final: Was Lionel Messi Really a Disappointment in Brazil or Have We Just Become Numb to His Genius? |last=Bate |first=Adam |date=16 July 2014 |publisher=[[Sky Sports]] |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190322134825/https://www.skysports.com/football/news/15241/9381511/world-cup-final-was-lionel-messi-really-a-disappointment-in-brazil-or-have-we-just-become-numb-to-his-genius |archive-date=22 March 2019 |access-date=14 March 2019}}</ref> A special red-coloured variation, "Telstar Mechta", was used for the knockout stage of the tournament. The word ''mechta'' (Russian: [[:wikt:мечта#Russian|мечта]]) means "dream" or "ambition".<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/44620577 |title=World Cup 2018: New ball to be used for knockout stage |date=6 June 2018 |publisher=BBC Sport |access-date=14 March 2019 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180629105225/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/44620577 |archive-date=29 June 2018}}</ref>

Goalkeepers noted that the ball was slippery and prone to having unpredictable trajectory.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Teather |first=Jamie |url=https://www.standard.co.uk/sport/football/worldcup/fifa-world-cup-2018-ball-adidas-telstar-18-football-a-problem-for-goalkeepers-a3864336.html |title=World Cup ball: Adidas Telstar 18 'a problem for goalkeepers' |date=16 June 2018 |work=London Evening Standard |access-date=17 June 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180617165321/https://www.standard.co.uk/sport/football/worldcup/fifa-world-cup-2018-ball-adidas-telstar-18-football-a-problem-for-goalkeepers-a3864336.html |archive-date=17 June 2018}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Price |first=Steve |url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/steveprice/2018/03/28/why-nike-contract-could-hurt-englands-preparations-for-russia-2018-world-cup/ |title=Why Nike Contract Could Hurt England's Preparations For Russia 2018 World Cup |date=28 March 2018 |work=[[Forbes]] |access-date=17 June 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180618003518/https://www.forbes.com/sites/steveprice/2018/03/28/why-nike-contract-could-hurt-englands-preparations-for-russia-2018-world-cup/ |archive-date=18 June 2018}}</ref> In addition, two Telstar 18 balls popped in the midst of a first-round [[2018 FIFA World Cup Group C#France vs Australia|match]] between [[France national football team|France]] and [[Australia men's national soccer team|Australia]], leading to further discussions over the ball's performance.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.news.com.au/sport/football/world-cup/adidas-and-world-cup-ball-have-a-deflating-moment-or-three-is-messi-really-the-goat/news-story/8af914537162d38190e1a71d42c7f180 |title=World Cup 2018: Adidas has a problem with the Telstar 18, is Messi the GOAT? |newspaper=News.com.au |date=21 June 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190626192105/https://www.news.com.au/sport/football/world-cup/adidas-and-world-cup-ball-have-a-deflating-moment-or-three-is-messi-really-the-goat/news-story/8af914537162d38190e1a71d42c7f180 |archive-date=26 June 2019 |access-date=14 March 2019}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://en.as.com/en/2018/06/16/football/1529146315_082887.html |title=Telstar 18 balls burst during France |date=16 June 2018 |website=AS.com |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190619193326/https://en.as.com/en/2018/06/16/football/1529146315_082887.html |archive-date=19 June 2019 |access-date=14 March 2019}}</ref>

===Music===
{{Main|Live It Up (Nicky Jam song)}}

The official song of the tournament was "Live It Up", with vocals by [[Will Smith]], [[Nicky Jam]] and [[Era Istrefi]], released on 25 May 2018. Its music video was released on 8 June 2018.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/2018-fifa-world-cup-russiatm-official-song-live-it-up-to-be-performed-by-all-sta |title=2018 FIFA World Cup Russia Official Song 'Live It Up' to be performed by all-star line-up |date=23 May 2018 |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180529044910/https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/2018-fifa-world-cup-russiatm-official-song-live-it-up-to-be-performed-by-all-sta |archive-date=29 May 2018}}</ref>

==Controversies==
{{Main list|List of 2018 FIFA World Cup controversies}}

Thirty-three footballers who were alleged to be part of the [[Doping in Russia|Russian steroid programme]] are listed in the [[McLaren Report]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/42062974 |title=Fifa contacts McLaren over doping claims in football |date=21 November 2017 |publisher=BBC Sport |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171216150738/http://www.bbc.com/sport/football/42062974 |archive-date=16 December 2017}}</ref> On 22 December 2017, it was reported that FIFA had fired a doctor who had been investigating doping in Russian football.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Conn |first=David |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2017/dec/20/doctor-sacked-fifa-alleged-russian-football-doping-jiri-dvorak-gianni-infantino |title=Doctor sacked by Fifa was investigating alleged Russian football doping |date=20 December 2017 |work=The Guardian |access-date=22 December 2017 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171222095625/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2017/dec/20/doctor-sacked-fifa-alleged-russian-football-doping-jiri-dvorak-gianni-infantino |archive-date=22 December 2017}}</ref> On 22 May 2018, FIFA stated that the investigations concerning all Russian players named for the provisional squad of the FIFA World Cup in Russia had been completed, with the result that insufficient evidence was found to support anti-doping rule violations.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/update-on-the-investigations-following-the-mclaren-reports |title=Update on the investigations following the McLaren reports |date=22 May 2018 |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180522153257/http://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/update-on-the-investigations-following-the-mclaren-reports |archive-date=22 May 2018}}</ref> FIFA's medical committee also decided that Russian personnel would not be involved in performing drug testing procedures at the tournament, an action taken to reassure teams that samples would not be tampered with.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-soccer-worldcup-doping-fifa/russians-to-take-no-part-in-world-cup-drug-testing-fifa-idUSKCN1IP0UK |title=Russians to take no part in World Cup drug testing: FIFA |date=24 May 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180528052242/https://www.reuters.com/article/us-soccer-worldcup-doping-fifa/russians-to-take-no-part-in-world-cup-drug-testing-fifa-idUSKCN1IP0UK |archive-date=28 May 2018 |work=Reuters}}</ref>

Russia relaxed its visa rules during the World Cup, allowing Fan ID holders to enter and exit Russia without a visa through 31 December 2018. Traffickers exploited this system to bring foreign sex trafficking victims into the country, especially from Nigeria.<ref>{{Cite web |title=2021 Trafficking in Persons Report: Russia |url=https://www.state.gov/reports/2021-trafficking-in-persons-report/russia/ |access-date=2022-12-10 |website=[[United States Department of State]] |at=in the section titled TRAFFICKING PROFILE |language=en-US |archive-date=8 December 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221208133305/https://www.state.gov/reports/2021-trafficking-in-persons-report/russia/ |url-status=live}}</ref> [[Reuters]] had raised concerns about the victims' conditions, who had allegedly been forced into prostitution, with some of them enduring violent abuse.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Maria Vasilyeva |date=2019-03-06 |title=Traffickers Used Russia's World Cup To Enslave Us, Say Nigerian Women |url=https://www.themoscowtimes.com/2019/03/06/traffickers-used-russias-world-cup-to-enslave-us-say-nigerian-women-a64729 |website=[[The Moscow Times]] |publisher=Reuters |language=en |access-date=10 December 2022 |archive-date=10 December 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221210210527/https://www.themoscowtimes.com/2019/03/06/traffickers-used-russias-world-cup-to-enslave-us-say-nigerian-women-a64729 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=7 March 2019 |title=Traffickers farmed Russia's World Cup for sex slaves |language=en |work=[[Bangkok Post]] |agency=Reuters |url=https://www.bangkokpost.com/vdo/world/1640704/traffickers-farmed-russias-world-cup-for-sex-slaves |access-date=10 December 2022 |archive-date=17 April 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230417062851/https://www.bangkokpost.com/vdo/world/1640704/traffickers-farmed-russias-world-cup-for-sex-slaves |url-status=live}}</ref> Russian authorities were accused of doing little to fix  the issue, allegedly because many locals blamed the victims for falling into prostitution.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Gershkovich |first=Evan |date=2018-06-13 |title=Russia's World Cup Opens the Floodgates for Trafficked Sex Workers |url=https://www.themoscowtimes.com/2018/06/13/world-cup-opens-the-floodgates-for-trafficked-sex-workers-a61722 |website=[[The Moscow Times]] |language=en |access-date=10 December 2022 |archive-date=10 December 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221210210526/https://www.themoscowtimes.com/2018/06/13/world-cup-opens-the-floodgates-for-trafficked-sex-workers-a61722 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Roache |first=Madeline |date=2018-07-13 |title=Putin Doesn't Care about Sex Trafficking |url=https://foreignpolicy.com/2018/07/13/putin-doesnt-care-about-sex-trafficking-russia-nigeria-world-cup-soccer/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://archive.today/20221207080407/https://foreignpolicy.com/2018/07/13/putin-doesnt-care-about-sex-trafficking-russia-nigeria-world-cup-soccer/ |archive-date=2022-12-07 |website=[[Foreign Policy]] |language=en-US}}</ref>

===Response to Skripal poisoning===
In response to the March 2018 [[poisoning of Sergei and Yulia Skripal]], British prime minister [[Theresa May]] announced that no British ministers or members of the royal family would attend the World Cup, and issued a warning to any travelling England fans.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Osborne |first=Samuel |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/uk/politics/theresa-may-russia-statement-in-full-spies-expel-nerve-agent-salisbury-poisoning-a8255661.html |title=Theresa May's statement in full as she expels 23 Russian spies from Britain |date=14 March 2018 |access-date=14 March 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180314192928/http://www.independent.co.uk/news/uk/politics/theresa-may-russia-statement-in-full-spies-expel-nerve-agent-salisbury-poisoning-a8255661.html |archive-date=14 March 2018 |website=The Independent}}</ref> Russia responded to the comments from the UK Parliament claiming that the West are trying to "take the World Cup out of Russia".<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/world-europe-43609505 |title=Moscow official says West is trying to deny Russia World Cup |date=1 April 2018 |publisher=BBC News |access-date=1 April 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180401042313/http://www.bbc.co.uk/news/world-europe-43609505 |archive-date=1 April 2018}}</ref> The [[Ministry of Foreign Affairs (Russia)]] denounced [[Boris Johnson]]'s statements that compared the event to the [[1936 Summer Olympics|1936 Olympics]] held in [[Nazi Germany]] as "poisoned with venom of hate, unprofessionalism and boorishness" and "unacceptable and unworthy" parallel towards Russia, a "nation that [[World War II casualties of the Soviet Union|lost millions of lives in fighting Nazism]]".<ref>{{Cite news |last=Osborne |first=Samuel |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/uk/crime/salisbury-chemical-weapons-inspectors-international-arrive-investigate-nerve-agent-attack-a8267546.html |title=Sergei Skripal: Chemical weapons inspectors arrive in Salisbury to investigate nerve agent attack |date=21 March 2018 |work=The Independent |access-date=19 June 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180619063045/https://www.independent.co.uk/news/uk/crime/salisbury-chemical-weapons-inspectors-international-arrive-investigate-nerve-agent-attack-a8267546.html |archive-date=19 June 2018}}</ref>

==Critical reception==
[[File:2018 FIFA World Cup opening ceremony (2018-06-14) 11.jpg|thumb|Facilities–such as the refurbished [[Luzhniki Stadium]] (pictured)–were one aspect of Russia's perceived success.]]
At the close of the World Cup, Russia was widely praised for its success in hosting the tournament, with Steve Rosenberg of the [[BBC]] deeming it "a resounding [[public relations]] success" for Putin, adding: "The stunning new stadiums, free train travel to venues and the absence of crowd violence has impressed visiting supporters. Russia has come across as friendly and hospitable: a stark contrast with the country's authoritarian image. All the foreign fans I have spoken to are pleasantly surprised."<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/world-europe-44812175 |title=Is Russia the real winner of World Cup 2018? |access-date=18 July 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180717005829/https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/world-europe-44812175 |archive-date=17 July 2018 |publisher=BBC}}</ref>

Despite the British [[Foreign, Commonwealth and Development Office|Foreign Office]] and MPs repeatedly warning English football fans travelling to Russia of "racist or homophobic intimidation, hooligan violence and anti-British hostility",<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/44395380 |title=World Cup 2018: New report highlights MPs' concern for England fans in Russia |date=8 June 2018 |publisher=BBC News |access-date=19 June 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180619081515/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/44395380 |archive-date=19 June 2018}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Calder |first=Simon |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/travel/news-and-advice/russia-world-cup-2018-england-football-fans-harassment-spy-poisoning-foreign-office-warning-a8256786.html |title=World Cup 2018: England football fans warned of potential 'anti-British harassment' when travelling to Russia |date=15 March 2018 |work=The Independent |access-date=19 June 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180619064431/https://www.independent.co.uk/travel/news-and-advice/russia-world-cup-2018-england-football-fans-harassment-spy-poisoning-foreign-office-warning-a8256786.html |archive-date=19 June 2018}}</ref> fans who did travel said they received a warm welcome from ordinary citizens after arriving in Russia.<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Mendick |first1=Robert |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/world-cup/2018/06/18/england-fans-welcomed-open-arms-volgograd-ahead-opening-2018/ |title=England fans welcomed with open arms in Volgograd ahead of opening 2018 World Cup match against Tunisia |date=18 June 2018 |work=The Daily Telegraph |access-date=25 June 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180621133805/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/world-cup/2018/06/18/england-fans-welcomed-open-arms-volgograd-ahead-opening-2018/ |archive-date=21 June 2018 |last2=Luhn |first2=Alec}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Carroll |first=Oliver |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/world-cup/england-tunisia-world-cup-latest-volgograd-russia-praise-welcome-fans-trouble-a8405341.html |title=World Cup 2018: England fans praise welcome by Russian hosts as they celebrate first win |date=19 June 2018 |work=The Independent |access-date=19 June 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180619071312/https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/world-cup/england-tunisia-world-cup-latest-volgograd-russia-praise-welcome-fans-trouble-a8405341.html |archive-date=19 June 2018}}</ref>

FIFA president [[Gianni Infantino]] stated: "Everyone discovered a beautiful country, a welcoming country, that is keen to show the world that everything that has been said before might not be true. A lot of preconceived ideas have been changed because people have seen the true nature of Russia."<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.news.com.au/sport/football/world-cup/qatar-2022-will-be-held-in-november-and-december/news-story/baf0e65bc2bc75a1ee9d468298c2c4d9 |title=Qatar 2022 will be held in November and December |access-date=17 July 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180718210902/https://www.news.com.au/sport/football/world-cup/qatar-2022-will-be-held-in-november-and-december/news-story/baf0e65bc2bc75a1ee9d468298c2c4d9 |archive-date=18 July 2018 |agency=News.com.au}}</ref> Infantino has proclaimed Russia 2018 to be "the best World Cup ever." 98 per cent of the stadiums were sold out, there were 3&nbsp;billion viewers on TV around the world and 7&nbsp;million fans visited the fan fests.<ref>{{Cite news |url=http://www.goal.com/en/news/world-cup-2018-fifa-president-gianni-infantino-russia-best/h6kts8bsl7m01ex2r0j59qph5 |title=Gianni Infantino: World Cup 2018 the best ever |access-date=19 July 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180718174442/http://www.goal.com/en/news/world-cup-2018-fifa-president-gianni-infantino-russia-best/h6kts8bsl7m01ex2r0j59qph5 |archive-date=18 July 2018 |publisher=Goal.com}}</ref> It was the most viewed World Cup to date, and the third [[List of most-watched television broadcasts#By year|most viewed television broadcast]], surpassing the [[2008 Summer Olympics]].

